course: dictionary
lesson: 2609
title: Deprive and taking things away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To deprive someone of something means to take it away from them, or to not let them have it. «The storm deprived the village of electricity for three days.» The storm took the power away, so the village had no electricity. Deprive is a verb, so it shows an action: deprive, deprives, deprived, depriving.
explain: The word is often followed by «of», and the thing taken away comes after it. «The plant was deprived of light, so its leaves turned pale.» The plant could not get the light it needed to stay healthy.
explain: You will meet this word in science, health and fairness. «Ben felt grumpy all day because he was sleep-deprived.» Sleep-deprived means not having enough sleep. When you see deprive, ask yourself: what has been taken away?
fact: Deprive is a verb: deprive, deprives, deprived, depriving.
fact: «Deprive» means to take something away from someone or not let them have it.
fact: Deprive is usually followed by «of», as in «deprived of food».
fact: «Sleep-deprived» means not having enough sleep.
fact: «Deprived area» (UK) means a poor neighbourhood, where people often lack money and things they need.
fact: A good check is to swap in «took away»: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «The storm deprived the village of electricity.» What does deprived mean here? | Gave a lot of | Washed clean | Lit up | *Took away
exercise: «The plant was deprived of light.» What was the plant deprived of? | A bee | A new pot | Its leaves | *Light
exercise: Which word means not having enough sleep? | Sleep-ready | *Sleep-deprived | Sleep-wrapped | Sleep-loud
test: «The teacher deprived the class of playtime.» What does deprive mean here? | *Took away | Cheered for | Waited for | Gave extra
test: Depriving people of their rights means | *Taking their rights away | Asking them to vote | Writing down new rules | Giving them more rights
exam: «Her cat was deprived of its favourite toy.» What does this mean? | *Its favourite toy was taken away | It was given a bigger toy | It found a new toy | It played with the toy all day
